              "failure": {
                "kind": "verification_failed",
                "failure_class": "bad_output",
                "evidence": "review changes_requested: duetflow/collector.py:32 passes each Spotify played_at through models.to_iso(), which truncates fractional seconds. Given two valid plays of the same track at 2026-01-01T10:00:00.100Z and 2026-01-01T10:00:00.900Z, collect() reports and stores only one row because both natural keys become 2026-01-01T10:00:00+00:00. This loses a distinct Play and violates the requirement that a Collection stores its plays.; duetflow/app.py:20-23 and 167-169 still route the public `duetflow collect` command to cmd_not_yet. Running `duetflow collect` at this phase exits 2 with `arrives in phase 02-collector-schema` instead of performing the Collection that docs/PLAN.md assigns to this command and says to run manually until phase 08, so the implemented collector is not usable through the project's public entry point.; duetflow/collector.py:12-41 never reads collection.max_collection_gap_minutes and has no warning path. After more than the configured 90 minutes between successful Collections, it silently proceeds, even though docs/ROADMAP.md:126-130 and docs/PLAN.md:296-300 require the Collector to warn because the 50-item ceiling may already have discarded plays; this makes silent, biased history loss indistinguishable from a healthy Collection.; duetflow/spotify.py:148-167 adds the first data-endpoint client but immediately converts every HTTP error to SpotifyError. For 401, 429 with Retry-After, and 503 inputs, the method makes exactly one request and aborts; docs/PLAN.md:249-256 requires one token refresh/retry on 401, honoring Retry-After and distinguishing quota exhaustion on 429, and exponential-backoff retries for 5xx. This risky network/auth change therefore makes transient rate limits/server failures lose a Collection window and cannot recover an expired access token.",
